//! THE WIRE RECORDS (spec §2.1, §2.2, §2.5) — the buffers the kernel WRITES and
//! userspace READS, and the offsets both halves index them by.
//!
//! The wait record and its levels, the Clock/Timer records, the pipe records,
//! and the process-stats record. One declaration serves both halves, which is
//! the whole reason this package exists.
//!
//! Reached through the `sosabi` facade — see `lib.saw`.

// THE ONE EDGE THAT CROSSES A LEAF BOUNDARY. `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`,
// `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` and `PipeMsgMeta` are the pipe body's dimensions and its
// packed meta, and they are declared in `ops` because they sit under the last
// op-table banner in the file this module was cut out of — the banners were the
// cut lines. The dependency is ONE-WAY: nothing in `ops` names a record, so the
// leaf graph stays acyclic (DF-232e).
import sosabi.ops.{PIPE_BODY_BYTES, PIPE_MSG_HANDLES, PipeMsgMeta}

// =============================================================================
// The wait record (spec §2.2, as amended by design 178 M2 unit 3 rider 3, and
// grown a BODY by design 10 ruling 11(a) — sawos design 15, M4 unit 3)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Waiter.Wait` answers with a RECORD the kernel COPIES OUT into a buffer the
// caller supplies, not with registers. That is what lets the answer carry a
// per-waitable payload without the ABI growing a register per object kind: a
// Pipe's readiness is not a Timer's is not an Event's, and a fixed register
// pair would have had to be the union of all of them forever.
//
// THE KEY IS UNIVERSAL AND IS FACTORED OUT. Every waitable answers with the
// word its attacher supplied (§2.2: it can directly encode the waiting task's
// identity, so the kernel neither interprets it nor constrains it); only the
// PAYLOAD varies by kind. So the record is `key, tag, payload...` and a reader
// gets the key without knowing what fired.
//
// THE WIRE FORMAT IS PUBLIC AND FIXED-WIDTH-BY-WORD: word 0 the key, word 1 the
// tag, word 2 onward the payload. It is machine words rather than fixed 32-bit
// fields because it never leaves the machine — unlike `sosimg`, which is a file
// two profiles must both read (design 47's discipline applies there and not
// here), this crosses one privilege boundary inside one address space.

/// Which kind of waitable a record describes — the record's TAG WORD.
///
/// Raw-backed (design 145's wire idiom): every case states its number, so a
/// reorder cannot renumber them, and the userspace decode reads it through
/// `from(raw:)`, which is total. The tag SPACE is extensible: §2.2 and §8
/// already name the kinds that follow (a Pipe readable or reply-ready, a
/// Timer fired, an Interrupt asserted, a Thread or Process exited), and each
/// takes the next number without moving this one.
///
/// AN UNKNOWN TAG IS A PANIC in the decode, not a `None` a caller handles. The
/// kernel WROTE it, out of this same declaration — so a tag userspace cannot
/// name is a kernel/`sos` skew, which is a bug in the pair rather than data
/// from an untrusted source. Never-hide says it should be loud.
///
/// **THE EXTENSIBILITY PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS COLLECTED BY `Process = 3`** (sawos
/// design 8, M3 unit 5.5): the fourth tag took the next number, moved none of
/// the three, and broke every exhaustive `WaitPayload` match in the tree until
/// each said what it does about a death — which is the raw backing and the
/// exhaustive match doing exactly what this docstring promised they would.
/// **AND COLLECTED FOUR MORE TIMES BY M4 UNIT 3** (sawos design 15, on design 10
/// rulings 8 and 11(a)): the four pipe arms took the next four numbers, moved
/// none of the four above them, and broke every exhaustive `WaitPayload` match
/// in the tree until each said what it does about a pipe.
public enum WaitTag: UInt {
    case Event = 0,
    case Interrupt = 1,
    case Timer = 2,
    /// A PROCESS ENDED, any cause (spec §8; sawos design 8). The payload is the
    /// §8 status word — `process_status_word(kind, code)` — so the `Exited` /
    /// `Faulted` distinction rides vocabulary that already shipped rather than
    /// a second tag per ending.
    case Process = 3,
    /// **A PipeOutlet IS READABLE** — the server end's level (spec §2.2's
    /// waitable list; sawos design 15). The payload is an `OutletLevel`: `Msg`
    /// while anything is staged, `PeerGone` when the ring is dry AND every
    /// inlet handle is gone. Drain-then-terminal, so `Msg` wins while there is
    /// anything left to hand over (design 230's close-drains-first rule).
    case Readable = 4,
    /// **A PipeInlet HAS ROOM TO POST** — the client end's level (design 10
    /// ruling 8). The payload is an `InletLevel`: `Room` while the staging ring
    /// has a free slot, `PeerGone` when every outlet handle is gone. A plain
    /// level, not a terminal one — consume clears nothing, and the level's end
    /// is the poster's own next post.
    case Room = 5,
    /// **A PipeReply's ANSWER HAS ARRIVED** — and this is the tag that made the
    /// record grow (design 10 ruling 11(a)). The payload is a `ReplyLevel`, and
    /// a `Data` delivery carries the reply BYTES in the record's body region:
    /// the wait itself is the delivery, so the composed client never calls
    /// `resolve` on the success path.
    case Reply = 6,
    /// **A PipeRequest's CLAIM WAS ABANDONED** — the server's cancellation
    /// watcher (spec §2.1's "the `PipeRequestHandle` is WAITABLE for
    /// abandoned"). No payload: the fact IS the notification, and it is
    /// TERMINAL, because a reply column that reached zero can never rise.
    case Abandoned = 7,
    /// **THE DELIVERY WAS THE TAKE** (`designs/010` ruling 11(d); sawos design
    /// 18) — a persistent-consuming outlet attachment being HANDED a message
    /// rather than told there is one. The record carries the packed meta, the
    /// obligation the kernel minted at the delivery, the message's handles and
    /// its bytes.
    ///
    /// **IT IS A SEPARATE TAG FROM `Readable`, AND THE SPLIT IS WHAT A TAG IS
    /// FOR**: the two say different things about the RECORD's shape, so a decode
    /// telling them apart by looking at a handle slot would be reading the
    /// payload to find out what the payload is. `Readable` still means there is
    /// something to take, which is what a borrowing `add(outlet:)` delivers.
    case Message = 8,
}

/// Words in a wait record's HEADER: key, tag, payload.
///
/// **IT WENT BACK TO THREE IN M4 UNIT 4** (design 18, amended at the STOP). Unit
/// 3 added a fourth word for the body's LENGTH; the packed meta carries that
/// number in a BYTE now, beside the kind bytes it has to travel with anyway, so
/// the word is gone and the record is smaller than it was between the two units.
/// A length that fits in a byte should not spend a machine word.
public static WAIT_HEADER_WORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the boundary.
public static WAIT_KEY_WORD: Int = 0
public static WAIT_TAG_WORD: Int = 1
public static WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD: Int = 2

/// Handle words in a wait record: one REQUEST slot, then one per message slot
/// (design 18; `designs/010` ruling 11(d)'s delivery-as-take needs the first and
/// ruling 5's rendezvous needs the rest).
///
/// The request slot is FIRST and is the one whose kind needs no byte: a wait
/// record's request slot is a `PipeRequest` or it is empty, so the word being
/// non-zero is the whole of what a decode has to ask. `PipeMsgMeta`'s kind bytes
/// describe the four that follow it, where the kind genuinely varies.
public static WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS: Int = 1 + PIPE_MSG_HANDLES

/// Where each handle slot sits inside the record's handle region.
public static WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT: Int = 0
public static WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0: Int = 1

/// **THE BUFFER A `Waiter.Wait` CALLER MUST SUPPLY** — the header, the packed
/// meta, the handle region, then a body region big enough for the largest
/// delivery there is (sawos design 15, grown by design 18).
///
/// **HANDLES BEFORE BYTES — THE FIXED-PREFIX / VARIABLE-TAIL RULE** (user-ruled
/// Sep 2). Everything whose size is fixed comes first and the body is the tail,
/// so EVERY copy-out is a CONTIGUOUS PREFIX: a delivery that carries handles and
/// no data writes the prefix and stops, and never pays for a 128-byte hole it
/// would have had to skip over if the body sat in the middle. Every handle word
/// and every kind byte therefore sits at an offset the decode knows without
/// reading anything first.
///
/// **AND THE COPY IS STILL SIZED TO THE DELIVERY** (ruled Sep 1, user, and
/// unchanged by the growth). A parked waiter cannot predict which arm fires, so
/// the BUFFER holds the biggest answer any of its attachments could give; the
/// KERNEL writes only what the delivery needs. Two prefix lengths, and the meta
/// is what tells them apart:
///
///   - a WORD-PAYLOAD delivery — an Event, an Interrupt, a Timer, a death, a
///     room level, an abandoned claim, a readable outlet nobody is taking
///     through — writes `wait_meta_end_bytes()` and stops. All five meta bytes
///     are zero, so a decode reads no handle word and no body byte.
///   - a MESSAGE delivery — a reply carrying data or handles, and ruling 11(d)'s
///     delivery-as-take — writes `wait_prefix_bytes()` plus the meta's `len`.
///
/// So a Timer waiter pays for the growth in buffer space and never in copy time,
/// which is the sentence unit 3 wrote here and unit 4 keeps.
///
/// A STRUCT RATHER THAN A WORD ARRAY, and the reason is alignment — now twice
/// over. Declaration order IS the layout (design 58), so the header words put the
/// struct at machine-word alignment, the five meta BYTES sit in the padding the
/// word-aligned handle region needs anyway, and the body lands at exactly
/// `wait_body_offset()`. **THAT PADDING IS WHERE THE PER-PROFILE ANSWER LIVES**:
/// the meta occupies one machine word on the 64-bit profile and two on the
/// 32-bit one, and it is the natural ABI that says so rather than a number
/// anybody computed.
public struct WaitBuffer {
    public header: [UInt; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
    public meta: PipeMsgMeta,
    public handles: [UInt; WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS],
    public body: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

/// Bytes in a wait record's HEADER — where the packed meta starts.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static`, which is a language limitation rather than a
/// choice: `sizeof<UInt>()` does not fold in a static initializer, even though
/// design 186's own diagnostic lists it among the things that do (DF-178f). The
/// arithmetic folds perfectly well in an ordinary expression, so the constant is
/// spelled where it is used and this is the one place it is written.
public func wait_header_bytes() -> Int {
    WAIT_HEADER_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// Bytes a WORD-PAYLOAD delivery writes: the header and the packed meta, with
/// whatever padding the handle region's alignment adds.
///
/// **IT IS THE SHORT PREFIX, AND IT INCLUDES THE PADDING ON PURPOSE.** The
/// alternative — stopping at the meta's last real byte — would leave a copy
/// length that differs from an offset the caller can compute, and would buy back
/// at most seven bytes on a path that is already the cheapest one. Ending at the
/// handle region's start keeps one arithmetic for both halves.
public func wait_meta_end_bytes() -> Int {
    // THE PARENTHESES ARE LOAD-BEARING: a binary expression does not wrap in
    // Saw unless brackets already enclose it, and a newline ends a statement —
    // so the un-bracketed spelling parses as a discarded subtraction followed
    // by a NEGATED tail, which is a silently wrong answer rather than an error.
    (sizeof<WaitBuffer>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES
     - WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS * sizeof<UInt>())
}

/// Bytes a MESSAGE delivery writes before its body — the whole fixed prefix, and
/// the offset the body region starts at.
public func wait_prefix_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<WaitBuffer>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES
}

/// Bytes in a wait record BUFFER on THIS machine — the minimum capacity a caller
/// must give `Waiter.Wait`, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// **IT MOVED IN M4 UNIT 3 AND AGAIN IN UNIT 4, AND THE vDSO DISCIPLINE IS WHAT
/// MAKES THAT FREE** (design 10 ruling 11(a), then design 18): every caller's
/// buffer comes out of `sos.pipe`'s own frame, so the number is written once here
/// and read once there. The two raw-altitude waiters in the suite over-provision
/// deliberately and are checked against this number rather than tracking it.
public func wait_record_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<WaitBuffer>()
}

static_assert(sizeof<WaitBuffer>() >= WAIT_HEADER_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
                                      + sizeof<PipeMsgMeta>()
                                      + WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS * sizeof<UInt>()
                                      + PIPE_BODY_BYTES,
              "a wait buffer must hold the header, the meta, every handle slot AND a whole message body")

// THE FIXED PREFIX IS A WHOLE NUMBER OF MACHINE WORDS, which is what makes "a
// contiguous prefix ending at the body's length" an arithmetic rather than a
// hope: the handle region is word-aligned and word-sized, so everything ahead of
// the body is too, and the two profiles differ only in how many words the meta's
// five bytes occupy — TWO on riscv32, ONE on arm64.
static_assert((sizeof<WaitBuffer>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES) % sizeof<UInt>() == 0,
              "a wait record's fixed prefix must be a whole number of machine words")

// AND THE META REALLY FITS THE REGION THE SHORT PREFIX ENDS AT. This is the one
// that would catch a `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` raised past what the padding absorbs.
static_assert(sizeof<WaitBuffer>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES
                  - WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS * sizeof<UInt>()
              >= WAIT_HEADER_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>() + sizeof<PipeMsgMeta>(),
              "the packed meta must fit between the header and the handle region")

// **THE DECODED FORMS ARE NOT HERE ANY MORE — THEY LIVE IN `sos.pipe`** (design
// 18, amended at the STOP; user-ruled Sep 2). `WaitPayload`, `WaitResult`,
// `PipeMsg` and `ReplyDelivery` were declared in this module by unit 3, which
// was right for as long as a delivery carried only WORDS AND BYTES. Unit 4 puts
// HANDLES in a message, and a handle at the typed tier is an owning `sos`
// wrapper — an `Event`, a `PipeReply`, a `Memory` — which THIS module can never
// name: `sos` depends on `sosabi` and nothing depends the other way, which is
// the whole of why the numbers can be kernel-internal.
//
// So the four moved up and `decode_wait` and `Waiter.wait` moved with them, to
// the one module that can name every wrapper a message may carry. WHAT STAYS
// HERE IS THE WIRE: the tag, the three level enums, the record's word offsets
// and `WaitBuffer` itself — everything the KERNEL writes, which is exactly the
// half this module exists to keep the two sides agreeing about.

/// A `PipeOutlet`'s readable level, decoded (sawos design 15).
///
/// **DRAIN-THEN-TERMINAL IS THE ORDER, so `Msg` WINS while anything is staged**
/// (design 230's close-drains-first rule, which `PipeOutletOp.Take` already
/// follows): a sender exiting mid-conversation does not un-send what it sent, so
/// a waiter is told `PeerGone` only once the ring is dry as well.
public enum OutletLevel: UInt {
    case Msg = 0,
    case PeerGone = 1,
}

/// A `PipeInlet`'s room-to-post level, decoded (design 10 ruling 8).
///
/// **`PeerGone` WINS HERE, and the asymmetry with `OutletLevel` is the ops'**:
/// `PipeInletOp.Post` asks about the outlet column BEFORE it asks about the
/// ring, because nobody being able to take is the stronger fact — a free slot
/// would be a place to put a message with no future. So a waiter learns which
/// of the two it woke for without a probe post, which is what ruling 8 asks the
/// payload word for.
public enum InletLevel: UInt {
    case Room = 0,
    case PeerGone = 1,
}

/// Which of `ReplyDelivery`'s two shapes a reply record carries — the wire
/// discriminator that its decoded form is built from.
public enum ReplyLevel: UInt {
    case Data = 0,
    case PeerClosed = 1,
}


// =============================================================================
// The Clock and Timer records (spec §2's Clock/Timer rows; design 232 unit 1)
// =============================================================================
//
// TWO MORE RECORDS, AND ONE OF THEM GOES THE OTHER WAY. `Clock.Now` answers
// with a copy-OUT record on the `Waiter.Wait` precedent; `Timer.Arm` takes its
// arguments through a copy-IN record, which is the funnel's mirror twin and is
// built here because M4's Pipe `send` needs exactly this shape for a message
// body (§2.1). Timer.Arm is its small first consumer precisely as Waiter.Wait
// was for copy-out.
//
// WHY EITHER NEEDS A RECORD AT ALL: a NANOSECOND COUNT IS 64 BITS. Anything
// narrower wraps in four seconds, which is not a clock. One of the two profiles
// has 32-bit registers, so one time is two words and `arm`'s pair is four —
// past the three argument registers §5.7 gives an op, and past what a single
// value register can answer with.
//
// THESE RECORDS ARE INDEXED IN DOUBLEWORDS, NOT MACHINE WORDS, and that is the
// one way they differ from the wait record above. A wait record's fields are a
// KEY, a TAG and a PAYLOAD — all of them machine-word-shaped things (a task
// identity, a line number, an accumulated word), so it follows the register.
// These fields are TIMES, and a time is 64 bits on every machine SOS will ever
// run on, so following the register would make the layout differ per profile
// for no reason. Both still stay inside one address space, so design 47's
// wire-format discipline still does not apply — what applies is that the field
// width is a property of the QUANTITY rather than of the machine.

/// Doublewords in a `Clock.Now` answer: the reading, and nothing else.
public static CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 1

/// Where the reading sits. Named rather than written, on both sides.
public static CLOCK_NOW_TIME_SLOT: Int = 0

/// Bytes in a `Clock.Now` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give the
/// op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f) and folds
/// perfectly well in an ordinary expression.
public func clock_now_record_bytes() -> Int {
    CLOCK_NOW_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

/// Doublewords in a `Timer.Arm` request: the first deadline, then the interval.
public static TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 2

/// Where each lives.
///
/// `AFTER` IS RELATIVE AND `INTERVAL` IS NOT A TIME AT ALL, which is the one
/// thing a reader has to carry away from this layout. The first field says how
/// long from NOW the first fire is — relative, because an absolute first
/// deadline would make every caller read the clock first and race between the
/// two syscalls. The second is a PERIOD, and zero means one-shot: a timer that
/// fires once and disarms itself.
public static TIMER_ARM_AFTER_SLOT: Int = 0
public static TIMER_ARM_INTERVAL_SLOT: Int = 1

/// Bytes in a `Timer.Arm` record — exactly what the caller must hand over, and
/// what the kernel checks the length against.
public func timer_arm_record_bytes() -> Int {
    TIMER_ARM_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

// =============================================================================
// The pipe records (spec §2.1; sawos designs 13 and 14)
// =============================================================================
//
// TWO PUBLISHED NUMBERS AND ONE RECORD, and they are here rather than in
// `kcore.limits` for the reason `wait_record_bytes()` is here: THEY ARE THE
// CONTRACT BETWEEN THE TWO HALVES. Design 10 ruling 6 asks for the limits as
// named build defines and does not say which module — `kcore.limits` is where a
// SLAB dimension belongs, and a slab dimension is a fact about the kernel that
// userspace never needs. `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is the other kind: a `Post` caller
// must not name more and a `Take` caller must supply at least that much, so it
// is exactly the published minimum a copy funnel's two sides have to agree on.
// One declaration, in the module both halves compile against.
//
// `PIPE_INFLIGHT` STAYED IN `kcore.limits` on the same cut, and it is the honest
// half of it: how deep the staging ring is derives from `MAX_THREADS`, and a
// caller that meets a full ring is told `WouldBlock` without ever having to know
// the number.

// **`PIPE_BODY_BYTES` USED TO BE DECLARED HERE**, and it moved to just above the
// wait record (sawos design 15, M4 unit 3). Nothing about it changed except its
// line: ruling 11(a) put a BODY REGION of exactly that many bytes into every
// wait-record buffer, an array length is a constant expression, and statics fold
// in DECLARATION ORDER — so the number has to be above the first thing that
// reads it. The reasoning for why it is in `sosabi` at all is unchanged and is
// carried with it.

/// Words in a `Process.PipeCreate` answer: the inlet handle, then the outlet.
///
/// **MACHINE WORDS, NOT DOUBLEWORDS**, and the rule is the boot-handle record's:
/// a HANDLE is a machine-word-shaped thing, so the layout follows the register.
/// Only a TIME is a fixed 64 bits on every profile.
public static PIPE_CREATE_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 2

/// Where each end's handle lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of
/// the boundary. The ORDER is inlet-then-outlet — client end first, the way the
/// pair reads in every sentence about it.
public static PIPE_CREATE_INLET_WORD: Int = 0
public static PIPE_CREATE_OUTLET_WORD: Int = 1

/// Bytes in a `Process.PipeCreate` record — the minimum capacity a caller must
/// give the op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f).
public func pipe_create_record_bytes() -> Int {
    PIPE_CREATE_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// **A DELIVERED MESSAGE** — what a `Take`, a fused `Call`'s reply and a
/// `Resolve` all copy out (spec §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)`, both directions;
/// sawos design 18).
///
/// **ONE SHAPE FOR EVERY DELIVERED MESSAGE, AND IT READS LIKE THE WAIT RECORD'S
/// TAIL.** Unit 2's take answered `{len, request}` in one record and the BODY in
/// a second buffer; a message carries HANDLES now, so the two buffers would have
/// become three. Instead the delivery is one record in the wait record's own
/// order — the packed meta, then the handle words, then the body — so a caller
/// reading a message out of a `take` and a caller reading one out of a `wait`
/// are reading the same layout, and the kernel writes it with the same
/// arithmetic.
///
/// **HANDLES BEFORE BYTES**, so every copy-out is a contiguous prefix ending at
/// `meta.len` (user-ruled Sep 2). A handles-only message pays for no part of the
/// 128-byte body region, and a data-only message writes four zero kind bytes and
/// the handle words it does not use are simply not written — `meta.kinds` is
/// what says so.
public struct PipeMsgRecord {
    public meta: PipeMsgMeta,
    public handles: [UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
    public bytes: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

/// **A DELIVERED MESSAGE PLUS THE OBLIGATION TO ANSWER IT** — `PipeOutlet.Take`'s
/// record (spec §2.1; sawos design 14, reshaped by design 18).
///
/// The request handle comes FIRST because it is the one word whose kind never
/// varies: a take's obligation is a `PipeRequest` or the take did not happen, so
/// it needs no kind byte and it reads best beside the length that follows it.
/// Everything after it is an ordinary `PipeMsgRecord`, which is what makes a
/// server able to hand the message half onward without repacking it.
public struct PipeTakeRecord {
    public request: UInt,
    public msg: PipeMsgRecord,
}

/// Bytes in a delivered-message record, and where its body region starts.
///
/// FUNCTIONS AND NOT `static`s for `pipe_create_record_bytes`' reason (DF-178f):
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer and folds perfectly well
/// in an ordinary expression — and in a `static_assert`, which is what pins the
/// kernel's prefix-only twins against these.
public func pipe_msg_record_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<PipeMsgRecord>()
}

public func pipe_msg_body_offset() -> Int {
    sizeof<PipeMsgRecord>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES
}

/// Bytes in a `PipeOutlet.Take` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give
/// the op — and where ITS body region starts.
public func pipe_take_record_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<PipeTakeRecord>()
}

public func pipe_take_body_offset() -> Int {
    sizeof<PipeTakeRecord>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES
}

// The two prefixes are whole numbers of machine words for `WaitBuffer`'s reason,
// which is what makes the contiguous-prefix copy an arithmetic on both profiles.
// The expressions are written out rather than reaching for the two functions
// above: a `sizeof` folds in a `static_assert` and a CALL does not.
static_assert((sizeof<PipeMsgRecord>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES) % sizeof<UInt>() == 0,
              "a message record's fixed prefix must be a whole number of machine words")
static_assert((sizeof<PipeTakeRecord>() - PIPE_BODY_BYTES) % sizeof<UInt>() == 0,
              "a take record's fixed prefix must be a whole number of machine words")

/// **A MESSAGE TO SEND** — the argument record `Post`, `Call` and `Reply` are
/// handed (spec §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)`; sawos design 18).
///
/// **IT GOES THE OTHER WAY — THIS IS A COPY-IN RECORD**, `TimerOp.Arm`'s door,
/// and it exists for the plainest reason §5.7 gives: a message has THREE things
/// to say now — a body, its length, and up to `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` handle words —
/// and a fused `Call` needs a fourth register for the reply's destination. Unit
/// 2's `post(body, len)` fitted the registers; `post(body, len, handles…)` does
/// not, on any of the three ops, so all three take the same record and the shape
/// is uniform rather than one-per-op.
///
/// **THE BODY STAYS AN ADDRESS RATHER THAN JOINING THE RECORD**, which is design
/// 17 deviation 3's decision inherited whole: a record holding the body itself
/// would make every send copy `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` in userspace before the trap,
/// where an address costs one word and the kernel's own copy-in is then the only
/// copy there is. The handle WORDS are different — there are four of them, they
/// are words, and there is nowhere else for them to ride.
///
/// A slot holding `NO_HANDLE` is an EMPTY slot. The kernel reads each non-empty
/// one out of the sender's own table, so a word naming nothing, a word without
/// the universal `Transfer` right and a word naming a kind no `BootHandleKind`
/// spells all TERMINATE the sender — each is a mistake only the caller could
/// have made — and the refusal happens before anything is staged.
public struct PipeSendRecord {
    public body: UInt,
    public len: UInt,
    public handles: [UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
}

/// Bytes in a send record — EXACTLY what the three ops must be handed. A
/// FUNCTION for `pipe_create_record_bytes`' reason (DF-178f).
public func pipe_send_record_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<PipeSendRecord>()
}

/// **`PipeRequest.ReplyWait`'s ARGUMENT record**: the waiter handle, then an
/// ordinary send record (sawos design 17, grown by design 18).
///
/// It is a send record with a WAITER in front of it, which is what the op is: a
/// reply with a built-in wait. Unit 3.5 spelled the three words out; unit 4 gave
/// a reply handles to carry, and rather than restate the four new words this
/// record now NESTS the one every other sending op takes — so `reply` and
/// `reply_wait` cannot drift about what a message is.
public struct PipeReplyWaitRecord {
    public waiter: UInt,
    public msg: PipeSendRecord,
}

/// Bytes in a `PipeRequest.ReplyWait` argument record — EXACTLY what the op
/// must be handed. A FUNCTION for `pipe_create_record_bytes`' reason (DF-178f).
public func pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<PipeReplyWaitRecord>()
}

// =============================================================================
// The process-stats record (spec §2's Process row; sawos design 16)
// =============================================================================
//
// **DOUBLEWORDS, NOT MACHINE WORDS, AND THE CLOCK RECORD'S REASON IS THIS
// RECORD'S REASON**: a COUNT is a property of the quantity, not of the machine.
// A 32-bit column on riscv32 would wrap after four billion traps and a 64-bit
// one on arm64 would not, so the same program would answer two different things
// about itself depending on which profile it was built for. Sixty-four bits on
// both, exactly as a nanosecond count is.
//
// **WHAT EACH COLUMN COUNTS, AND WHICH ONE A TEST MAY ASSERT.** The split is by
// the CAUSE CLASS the trap entry decides on (`ktrap`'s own three-way order),
// which is why it is the split that makes the numbers useful:
//
//   `syscalls`    every trap the process took through the syscall instruction —
//                 one per `ecall`/`svc`, whatever the op, whatever the answer,
//                 and ONE for a call that parked for a millisecond. So it is
//                 EXACT for a given program path and it is the column an
//                 assertion should read.
//   `interrupts`  every timer or device interrupt taken while this process was
//                 the current one. NONDETERMINISTIC by construction — the tick
//                 lands where it lands — so it is printed and never asserted,
//                 the timing-rows doctrine at a smaller scale.
//   `faults`      every other exception: an access fault, a misaligned access,
//                 an illegal instruction. Exact where a test provokes one on
//                 purpose, and normally zero, because a fault ends the process
//                 (§8) — so the interesting reader is a SUPERVISOR holding the
//                 dead child's handle, not the process itself.
//
// **A BAD SYSCALL COUNTS AS A SYSCALL AND NOT AS A FAULT.** A word naming no
// handle, an op the object does not have, a right that was not given: each of
// those ENDS the process (design 178's faults ruling), but the trap that carried
// it arrived through the syscall instruction, and this record's columns are keyed
// by how the machine entered the kernel rather than by how the kernel felt about
// it. Keying on the outcome instead would make `syscalls` un-assertable, because
// a caller cannot know in advance which of its calls the kernel will refuse.
//
// **ATTRIBUTION IS TO THE PROCESS CURRENT AT TRAP ENTRY**, decided before
// anything is dispatched, so a syscall that switches processes is charged to the
// one that made it and an interrupt that preempts is charged to the one that was
// running. Traps taken through the BOOT DOOR, before any process exists, are
// UNATTRIBUTED — the harness kernels that enter user mode with no object model
// have a running program and no process slot, and there is nowhere honest to put
// their count. `ktrap` says so at the site.
//
// **THE COLUMNS ZERO AT PROCESS CREATE**, both at root's boot-time reification
// and at `SystemOp.ProcessCreate`, so a reused slot never inherits its
// predecessor's traffic.

/// Doublewords in a `Process.Stats` answer: syscalls, interrupts, faults.
public static PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each column sits. Named rather than written, on both sides of the
/// boundary. The ORDER is the order the paragraph above reads them in, which is
/// also assertable-first.
public static PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT: Int = 0
public static PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT: Int = 1
public static PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a `Process.Stats` record — the minimum capacity a caller must give
/// the op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f).
public func process_stats_record_bytes() -> Int {
    PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>()
}

/// **THE THREE COLUMNS AS A RECORD** — the one declaration both doors read
/// (sawos design 37, M5 unit 7).
///
/// Declaration order IS the layout (design 58), so this struct and the
/// doubleword slots above are ONE description of ONE thing: `Process.Stats`
/// copies this record out, and the shared region below is an array of it. That
/// is what makes the v1 op a COMPATIBILITY FLOOR rather than a second surface —
/// the two answers cannot drift, because there is nothing for them to drift
/// between.
public struct StatsRow {
    public syscalls: UInt64,
    public interrupts: UInt64,
    public faults: UInt64,
}

static_assert(sizeof<StatsRow>()
              == PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS * sizeof<UInt64>(),
              "a stats row must be exactly the doublewords sosabi publishes")

/// Bytes in one row. A FUNCTION for `process_stats_record_bytes`' reason.
public func stats_row_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<StatsRow>()
}

// =============================================================================
// The shared stats region (sawos design 37, M5 unit 7; design 25 seed 1)
// =============================================================================
//
// **ONE STORAGE, TWO DOORS, NO MIRROR.** The three columns live HERE — in a
// kernel-owned, protection-granularity-aligned region — and `Process.Stats`
// READS this same storage. There is no second copy kept in step, because a
// second copy is a thing that can skew; what the op gives a caller is a
// snapshot COPIED OUT of the one storage, and what the region gives a caller is
// the storage itself, read-only.
//
// **THE POINT IS A `top` THAT COSTS NO SYSCALLS.** A supervisor maps the region
// once and reads every process's columns out of its own address space for the
// life of the machine — no trap per sample, no trap per process. That is the
// whole reason the region exists; `Process.Stats` remains the answer for a
// caller that wants one process's numbers and does not want a mapping.
//
// **ROWS ARE INDEXED BY PROCESS SLOT**, and the region has a row for every slot
// the machine can ever hand out. See `kcore.stats` for the capacity argument —
// the short version is that `alloc_process` scans to
// `min(capacity, hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS)`, so `PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` is a CEILING
// on the slot index and a region sized to it can never be outgrown, donated
// process slots (design 36) included.
//
// **TEARING IS DOCUMENTED, NOT HIDDEN.** A column is 64 bits and one profile's
// registers are 32, so a reader on riscv32 loads a doubleword as two words and
// the kernel can bump the low word between them. What a torn read yields is
// therefore a value BETWEEN two true readings of that counter, never a value
// the counter never held — the columns only ever increase, so the low word
// carrying or not carrying is the whole of the hazard, and the high word moves
// once every 2^32 traps. v1's answer is this paragraph plus monotonicity: a
// `top` renders a number that is at most one carry stale, which is what a `top`
// is for. A SEQLOCK is the refinement if a consumer ever needs exactness — an
// even/odd generation word per row, written around each bump — and it is
// deliberately NOT built, because no consumer needs it and a lock in the trap
// path costs every process to serve a reader that does not exist yet.
//
// **THE REGION IS NOT A `Memory`, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN.** No handle is minted
// for it: it is kernel storage, not allocatable RAM, so it must not wear the
// Split/donate machinery a `Memory` carries — splitting the stats page or
// donating it to a slab are not operations that could ever mean anything. What
// the caller gets is ACCESS WITHOUT POSSESSION: a protection row installed in
// its own domain and a `Mapping` naming that row, which is exactly enough to
// read the page and to give the access back.

/// What the region publishes about itself, at its own base.
///
/// **SELF-DESCRIBING ON PURPOSE.** A reader that has the page and this struct
/// needs nothing else: `rows_offset` says where row 0 starts and `row_bytes`
/// says how far apart they are, so a v2 that grows this header does not move
/// the rows out from under a v1 reader's arithmetic — it changes a number the
/// v1 reader already reads. `version` is what a reader checks first and refuses
/// on.
///
/// Every field is `UInt64` for the columns' own reason: the region is one
/// layout on both profiles, so nothing here follows the register.
public struct StatsRegionHeader {
    public version: UInt64,
    public rows: UInt64,
    public row_bytes: UInt64,
    public rows_offset: UInt64,
}

/// The layout version this build publishes and this build's readers accept.
public static STATS_REGION_VERSION: UInt64 = 1

/// Bytes in the header. A FUNCTION for `process_stats_record_bytes`' reason.
public func stats_region_header_bytes() -> Int {
    sizeof<StatsRegionHeader>()
}

/// Words in a `System.StatsRegionMap` copy-out record: the caller's own ROW
/// INDEX, and nothing else.
///
/// **MACHINE WORDS, NOT DOUBLEWORDS** — the pipe-create record's rule: a table
/// index is a register-shaped thing.
///
/// **THE MAPPING IS NOT IN HERE, AND THAT IS `map`'s SHAPE KEPT.** The op
/// answers with a `Mapping` handle in the value register exactly as
/// `MemoryOp.Map` does, and the ADDRESS comes from `MappingOp.Base` exactly as
/// it does there — one mechanism for "the address is the kernel's answer",
/// not two. This record carries the one thing `map` has no equivalent of.
///
/// **WHY A ROW INDEX IS OWED AT ALL.** Rows are indexed by process SLOT, and
/// until this op there was no way for a process to learn its own — slot
/// numbering is kernel-internal. A caller handed the page without it would hold
/// a table it cannot find itself in.
///
/// **IT IS WRITTEN BEFORE THE ROW IS INSTALLED, AND THAT IS NOT A PARTIAL
/// SUCCESS.** The index is a FACT ABOUT THE CALLER — process `p` occupies row
/// `p` — rather than a product of the mapping, so it is true whether or not the
/// install goes on to succeed. A caller that gets an error status simply did not
/// get a mapping; the number it was told is still its row.
///
/// It is the caller's OWN row and nobody else's. Learning which row ANOTHER
/// process occupies is a naming question this unit does not answer — see design
/// 37's As-built, which records it as the follow-on a general `top` needs.
public static STATS_REGION_MAP_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 1

/// Where the row index sits. Named rather than written, on both sides.
public static STATS_REGION_MAP_ROW_WORD: Int = 0

/// Bytes in a `System.StatsRegionMap` record — the minimum capacity a caller
/// must give the op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
public func stats_region_map_record_bytes() -> Int {
    STATS_REGION_MAP_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}
